A Heterogeneous Nano Metal-Organic Framework Fluorescence Probe for Highly Selective and Sensitive Detection of Hydrogen Sulfide in Living Cells
College of Chemistry, Chemical Engineering and Materials Science, Collaborative Innovation Center of Functionalized Probes for Chemical Imaging, Key Laboratory of Molecular and Nano Probes, Ministry of Education, Shandong Normal University, Jinan 250014, P. R. China. Corresponding author e-mail: tangb@sdu.edu.cn. Fax: (86)53186180017.
## Introduction
Hydrogen sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\) has attracted great attention from its recently established function as signal transmitter in living organisms.1,2 It has been regarded as the third important gaseous signaling molecule following nitric oxide (NO) and carbon monoxide (CO).1-4 The endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), mainly biosynthesized by enzymatic reactions5, has been found to be involved in many physiological processes, such as antioxidation,6 anti-inflammation7 and apoptosis8. In addition, abnormal concentrations of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) have also been correlated with many pathological processes, such as Alzheimer's disease9 and Down's syndrome10. In order to better understand the contributions of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in these processes, it is crucial to monitor the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) level with spatial and temporal information in living cells and organisms. However, due to the high reactive and diffusible properties of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), the traditional detection technologies, such as gas chromatography,11,12 colorimetric13,14 and electrochemical analysis15,16, are not appropriate for real-time detection. They often require complicated post-mortem process and/or even destruction of tissues or living cells. Fortunately, the fluorescence probe-based fluorescence imaging technology was just born at the right time. Owing to the advantages of high selectivity and sensitivity, and good compatibility for various bio-samples of this technology,17-19 the design and synthesis of fluorescence probes for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection has inspired great interest in this field.
Recently, a certain amount of turn-on type fluorescence probes for detection of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) have been reported. And the design strategies mainly rely on the typical reactive characteristics of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\)-mediated hydroxyl amide-, nitro- and azide-reduction,20-30 \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) participated in the
dual nucleophilic addition,31-36 the formation of copper sulfide precipitation,37-40 and thiolysis of dinitrophenyl ether.41,42 These unique reactive properties can efficiently distinguish \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) from other biological species, however, the study of novel \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) probes are still in need because of the complexity for studying underlined molecular events involved in signaling transduction, and other problems related to the probes, such as their resistance to thiol interference, response times (typically 20 min–2h), detection limit, reside quantity control of the probe in cells, the presence of toxic organic cosolvent, etc.
In last few decades, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a new type of porous coordination compounds, have been studied extensively. Base on the properties of controllable synthesis, structural diversity, porosity, high specific surface areas, rich in metal active site and flexibility of the pore size/wall modification, these materials have shown great potential applications in gas storage, catalysis, chemical sensing, optics and biological applications, etc.43-46 Attractively, the past studies also revealed that the size of MOFs can also be pulled down to the nano range, and these nanoscale MOFs often provide a significant performance improvement over their bulk crystals in these applications.47 For example, a recently reported nano-scaled MOF probe could recognize \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and cysteine over other amino acids.48 However, the nano-MOFs have never been used as selective fluorescence probes for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection so far.
Herein we intent to develop a novel type heterogeneous nano-MOF probe for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection. The key point of probe design is how to introduce the reactive site for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) into the framework. Considering the flexibility of functionalization for MOFs, the design can be achieved by introduction of the active metal centers, ligand functionalization or post-
modification, which presages the huge potential for MOFs in probe design. Here, we only selected a simple strategy by introducing reactive metal centers \((\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions) into the framework as the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\)-responding site, and synthesized a 3D porous nanoscale aluminum-copper mixed-metal MOF \(\{\mathrm{CuL[AlOH]_{2}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) (referred to as PAC, \(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{L} = \text{meso-tetra}(4-\text{carboxyl-phenyl})\) porphyrin) as a novel \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) fluorescence probe (Figure 1). Combining the unique properties of MOFs (such as porosity, high specific surface areas, abundant host-guest interactions, the inherent confinement effect within the pores and rich metal active sites) and advantages of nano-scale materials (such as easy to implement probe endocytosis, avoid exosmosis of small organic molecule probes together with increasing reservation amount of the probe), this biocompatible nano-MOF probe exhibits rapid response, excellent selectivity and hypotoxicity in in-situ detection of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), and represents the most sensitive fluorescence probe for selective \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection under physiological \(\mathrm{pH}\). In addition, the confocal imaging was achieved successfully in living cells.
## Experimental Section
Materials. The reagents were obtained from commercial sources and used without further purification. Pyrrole, 4-formylbenzoic acid, copper acetate, aluminum trichloride, sodium hydrosulfide, glutathione (GSH), cysteine (Cys), homocysteine (Hcy), hydrogen peroxide \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(30\%\) aqueous solution), Dulbecco's modified Eagle's medium (DMEM), 3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT), S-nitroso-N-acetyl-co-penicillamine (SNP, the source of NO) and other relevant inorganic compounds (KSCN, \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{NaClO}\), \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HPO}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}\)) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Shanghai, China). Hoechst 33342 was obtained from Beyotime Institute of Biotechnology (shanghai, china). Ultrapure water (18.2MU cm) was obtained from a Water Pro Water Purification System (Labconco Corporation, Kansas City, USA). HepG2 cells (Human hepatocellular liver carcinoma cells) and A549 cells (Human lung carcinoma cells) were purchased from the Committee on Type Culture Collection of the Chinese Academy of Sciences.
Physical Measurements. Powder X-ray Powder (PXRD) patterns were measured at room temperature (298 K) on a Bruker SMART APEX CCD-based diffractometer. \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra was taken on a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer (AVANCE 300, Bruker, Switzerland). The FT-IR (KBr
Physical Measurements. Powder X-ray Powder (PXRD) patterns were measured at room temperature (298 K) on a Bruker SMART APEX CCD-based diffractometer. \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra was taken on a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer (AVANCE 300, Bruker, Switzerland). The FT-IR (KBr pellet) spectrum was recorded (400-4000 cm\(^{-1}\) region) on a Nicolet Magna 750 FT-IR spectrometer. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were taken on a JEM-1011 electron microscope (JEOL, Japan) at an accelerating voltage of 100 kV. Energy dispersive spectroscopy mapping (EDS) analysis was carried out on SUPRATM 55 SEM. The fluorescence spectra were determined on a spectrofluorometer (FLS-920, Edingburgh). Absorbance was measured on a microplate reader (RT-6000, Rayto, Guangdong, China) in the MTT assay. Fluorescence kinetics was recorded on a Cary Eclipse spectrofluorometer (Varian, Australia) with a Xenon lamp. All pH measurements were carried out by a pH-3c digital pH-meter (Shanghai Lei Ci Device Works, Shanghai, China) with a combined glass-calomel electrode. The fluorescence images of cells were recorded by a confocal laser scanning microscope (TCS SP5, Leica, Wetzlar, Germany) with the excitation wavelength was 405 nm.
Synthesis of L ligand and nano-MOF PAC. The organic ligand [meso-tetra(4-carboxyl-phenyl)porphyrin, \(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{L}\)] was synthesized according to the literature. Firstly, 4-Formylbenzoic acid (3.0 g, 0.02 mol) was added to a solution of propionic acid (200 mL) in pyrrole (1.39 mL, 0.02 mol). Next, the mixture was heated to reflux for 1 h. After cooling, a brown-purple precipitate formed. The precipitate was isolated by filtration, washed with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\), and then dried under vacuum for 6 h. \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR (DMSO- \(d_{6}\) 300 MHz): 8.08 (m, 8H), 8.27 (m, 8H), 8.87 (br, 8H).
A mixture of \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.0125 mmol, 0.030 g), \(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{L}\) (0.063 mmol, 0.050 g), and cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) (1 mmol, 0.036 g) in water (5 mL) was stirred for 10 min at room temperature and then heated in a 23 mL Teflon-lined autoclave at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 16 hours. After being cooled to room temperature slowly, the purple nanoscale crystals (referred to as nano-precursor PA) were collected by centrifugal separation and washed with DMF, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and acetone in turn for several times, yield: \(39\%\). Then, a mixture of the activated PA (170°C under vacuum, overnight) (50 mg), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.2 mmol, 0.050 g) in DMF (5 mL) was stirred for 10 min at room temperature and then heated in a 23 mL Teflon-lined autoclave at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 hours. After being cooled to room temperature slowly, the sample were also collected by centrifugal separation and washed with DMF, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and acetone in turn for several times, yield: \(90\%\). The phase purity of the samples and the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) insertion rate at the centre of the porphyrin have been confirmed by PXRD, FT-IR and EDS.
Cell culture. HepG2 cells and A549 cells were maintained following the protocols provided by the American Type Tissue Culture Collection. HepG2 cells were incubated in high glucose Dulbecco's Modified Eagle Medium (DMEM, 4.5 g of glucose/L) supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS), \(\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}\) (2 g/L) and 1% antibiotics (penicillin/streptomycin, 100 U/mL). A procedure similar to that for HepG2 cells was followed to culture A549 cells, except the DMEM was replaced by 1640 Medium. Cultures were maintained in a humidified incubator in 5% \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/95\%\) air at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). HepG2 and A549 cells were both passed and plated on 18 mm glass coverslips in culture dish, and maintained one day at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 5% \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) before imaging.
Confocal imaging. After HepG2 cells being cultured on coverslips in culture dish for 24 h, the probe PAC (10 \(\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) was delivered into the cells in DMEM medium containing 10% FBS at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 1 h. Cells were then washed three times with
BBS buffer. Then the confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) with \(405~\mathrm{nm}\) excitation was performed with a He-Ne laser and the emission was collected between \(580{-}680\ \mathrm{nm}\). A procedure similar to that for HepG2 cells was followed to the confocal imaging of A549 cells, except the DMEM was replaced by 1640 Medium.
In the case of costaining HepG2 cells with PAC and a nucleus specific dye (Hoechst 33342), HepG2 cells were firstly co-incubated with DMEM containing \(10\%\) FBS, \(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC, and \(500~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) Hoechst 33342 at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\;\mathrm{h}\), then washed with BBS three times. Then the CLSM also with \(405~\mathrm{nm}\) excitation was performed and the emission was recorded between 580- \(680\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(410{-}480\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively.
MTT Assay. HepG2 \((10^{5}\;\mathrm{cell}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) were dispersed within replicate 96-well microtiter plates to a total volume of 200 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{well}^{-1}\). Plates were maintained at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/95\%\) air incubator for \(12~\mathrm{h}\). The cells were sequentially incubated with different concentrations of PAC (10, 30, 50, 100 and 200 \(\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for another \(24~\mathrm{h}\). MTT solution \((5.0~\mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\), BBS) was then added to each well. After \(4~\mathrm{h}\), the remaining MTT solution was removed, and \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of DMSO was added into each well to dissolve the formazan crystals. Absorbance was measured at \(490\;\mathrm{nm}\) in a TRITURUS microplate reader.
## Results and discussion
Preparation and Characterization of the Nano-MOF. The nanoscale aluminum-copper mixed-metal MOF PAC \(\{\mathrm{CuL[AlOH]}_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) was obtained from \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) metalation of the porphyrin ring within the rigid host structure of the fluorescence nano metal-organic framework \(\{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}[\mathrm{AlOH}]_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) (referred to as PA). The synthesis mainly benefits from the consideration that \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions are preferable to coordinated with nitrogen atoms in the nitrogen-bearing macrocyclic ligands. PAC maintained a stable non-interpenetrated 3D porous framework as PA, but the paramagnetic characteristics of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions quenched the ligand-based fluorescence completely as expected. When the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) appear, the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions are taken out from the porphyrin centers, and the luminophor nano-MOF PA is, thus, obtained simultaneously.
Nano-MOF PA was prepared according to the literature for bulk crystals of PA and other nanomaterials by adding surfactant cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), and the purity of the as-synthesized sample has been demonstrated by Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S1). Compound PAC was metalated by the reaction of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with activated nano PA in water under hydrothermal conditions at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The PXRD pattern for PAC obtained by the same condition is coincident with that for the as-synthesized sample of PA, suggesting that Cu metalation does not destroy the stable framework of PA (Figure S1). The energy-dispersive spectrum (EDS) gives an \(\mathrm{Al/Cu}\) ratio of 2, consistent with \(100\%\) occupancy of the porphyrin center by \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions (Figure S2). These all reveal that PAC also crystallizes in the space group Cmmm and exhibits non-interpenetrated three-dimensional porous frameworks. The \(\mathrm{Al(III)}\) center is six-coordinated by four equatorial carboxylate oxygen atoms from four different L ligands and two axial \(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}^{-}\)bridges. Adjacent metal ions are double bridged by these two \(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}^{-}\)bridges to generate a 1D \(\mathrm{Al(OH)O_{4}}\) chain. Each porphyrin linker with the center site occupied by \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions is coordinated to eight \(\mathrm{Al(III)}\) centers
through the four carboxylate groups and interconnects 1D \(\mathrm{Al(OH)O_{4}}\) chains to a complicated 3D porous framework.
Spectroscopic properties of PAC toward \(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}\). Subsequently, we investigated the spectroscopic properties of PAC. This nano probe with the average size of \(60~\mathrm{nm}\) being characterized by TEM (Figure S3), shows fine aqueous dispersibility and no need for toxic organic cosolvent. As can be seen from Figure 2, upon treatment of \(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) NaHS in aqueous solutions buffered at physiological \(\mathrm{pH}\) (20 mM Boric Acid-Borax butter, referred to as BBS, \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\)), a significant fluorescence enhancement was observed. To further evaluate the efficiency of the probe in the measurement of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) level, varying concentrations of NaHS \((0{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) were added to the solutions of PAC \((10~\upmu\mathrm{M})\). An excellent linear correlation between the fluorescence intensities and the added NaHS concentrations was observed (Figure S4). The detection limit for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) was found to be about \(16~\mathrm{nM}\), comparable to the most sensitive \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) sensing fluorescence probe HS–Cy that has been reported by our group. Beyond that, this new nano-MOF probe avoided the needs for improving the reaction rate by enhancing \(\mathrm{pH}\) (8.0) and the complexity of two excitation processes in the fluorescence imaging based on two-step reactive mechanism of \(\mathrm{HS-Cy}\). Therefore, this novel probe has become the most sensitive \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) probe under physiological \(\mathrm{pH}\) \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\).
Selectivity and reaction kinetic of PAC toward \(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}\). The experiments on selectivity were then carried out. As can be seen from Figure 3, compared with the large and immediate increment of fluorescence intensity upon addition of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) NaHS, almost no fluorescence increment upon addition of abundant biologically relevant glutathione (GSH, \(10\;\mathrm{\mM}\)), whose levels are often higher than other thiols in cells. We also examined other thiols and reactive individual oxygen/nitrogen species, such as Cys, Hcy, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ClO}^{-}\)and NO. Similarly, these species induced no obvious change in the fluorescence. Therein, such a large amount of interfering NO (1000 equiv in concentration) has not yet been tested. Additionally, other interfering inorganic anions \((\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{\ 2-},\ \mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}^{\ 2-}\), \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\ 2-}\), \(\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{\ 2-}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\)) were also examined and they all showed no or very limited fluorescence responses. These all experimental results emphasized the probe's high selectivity towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in the living cells.
To further establish the possibility of real-time imaging of PAC for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection, we examined the response time of PAC to NaHS in BBS buffered aqueous solution through reaction kinetic. As can be seen from Figure S5, a significant fluorescence enhancement was observed instantaneously. All this proved that PAC is able to detect \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) more rapidly and sensitively than most of the reported \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) probes (typically \(20\ \mathrm{min}-2\ \mathrm{h}\)).
Luminescence imaging of PAC in living cells.
In view of the above results, we have reason to believe that the novel fluorescence probe PAC is promising for applications in biological imaging. We then evaluated the capability of the probe to detect exogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in HepG2 cells (Human hepatocellular liver carcinoma cells). HepG2 cells were incubated with \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC in Dulbecco's modified Eagle's medium (DMEM) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) and then washed several times with Hanks' Balanced Salt Solutions (HBSS). From Figure 4a, we cannot observe any significant fluorescence cells with only \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC. The bright-field image was presented as Figure 4b. After \(50\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) NaHS (the donor of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\)) being co-incubated, a large intracellular fluorescence enhancement was observed (Figure 4c), corresponding to the bright-field image (Figure 4d). Simultaneously, a costaining experiment of HepG2 cells with PAC and a nucleus specific dye (Hoechst 33342) was conducted by a \(405\ \mathrm{nm}\) excitation with emission band of \(410-\) \(480\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(580-680\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. The good morphology of HepG2 cells after incubating HepG2 cells with PAC for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\) suggested satisfactory biocompatibility of the probe (Figure S6). In addition, a MTT assay with HepG2 cells was performed (Figure S7). All these results demonstrated that PAC was of low toxicity and high biocompatibility.
After the capability estimation of the probe PAC for exogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection in living cells, we further employed the probe to detect endogenic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in A549 cells (Human lung carcinoma cells), whose redox signaling has been found to be relative to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}^{52}\). In A549 cells, the relatively high contents of enzymes cystathionine \(\upbeta\)-synthase (CBS) and cystathionine \(\upgamma\)-lyase (CSE) are important for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) synthesis. It has also been demonstrated that NO could upregulate the activity of CSE and CBS. In addition, our experiment on selectivity emphasized the probe's high selectivity towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) over NO.
Therefore, we selected a commercial NO donor S-nitroso-N-acetyl-dl-penicillamine (SNP) to induce the generation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in A549 cells. A549 cells incubated with \(500\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) SNP for \(60\ \mathrm{min}\) were referred to as SNP-A549. As can be seen from Figure 4e-4f, after co-incubating SNP-A549 cells with \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC for another \(30\ \mathrm{min}\), significant fluorescence enhancement was observed. Inversely, while DL-propargylglycine (PPG, \(250\ \mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1}\)), an inhibitor for CBS and CSE, was added before the cells stimulated by SNP, no significant fluorescence cells were observed after coincubating SNP-A549 cells with \(10\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) PAC in the similar conditions with no inhibitor (Figure 4g-4h). The results indicated that this nano-MOF probe was able to detect \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in living cells.
## Conclusions
we have presented a \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) metalated non-interpenetrated 3D porous metal-organic framework and successfully employed this nano-MOF as a novel heterogeneous fluorescence probe for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection. As far as we known, nano-MOFs have never been used as a selective fluorescence probe for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) detection. Combining with the advantages of MOFs and nano-materials successfully, this biocompatible nano-MOF probe exhibits rapid response, excellent selectivity and hypotoxicity in in-situ detection of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), and has been proved to be the most sensitive \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) probe under physiological pH. Moreover, the confocal imaging was achieved successfully in living cells with this probe. This work, on the one hand, illustrates the great opportunity for the development of the interface between MOFs and the probe-based luminescence imaging techniques, and, on the other hand, demonstrates the great potential of finding novel and intriguing probe patterns for studying underline molecular events involved in signaling transduction.
